window.DIST_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Two RequestVote handlers for the same term run on different threads with no lock. What is the dangerous outcome?","o":["The node crashes with a ConcurrentModificationException","Both read votedFor as null and the node votes for both candidates, so two leaders can be elected in one term","The node votes for neither candidate","The term number is incremented twice"],"a":1,"w":"Check-then-act without atomicity: both threads pass the check before either writes. Each candidate counts the vote, which can give both a majority."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which of these creates a happens-before edge between thread A's write and thread B's read?","o":["Both threads run on the same CPU core","Thread A writes a plain field, then sleeps for one second","Thread A writes a volatile field, and thread B later reads that same field and sees the value","Thread B calls System.nanoTime() after thread A's write"],"a":2,"w":"A volatile write happens-before every subsequent read of that variable. Sleeping or timing proves nothing to the memory model."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A leader replicates each entry to 4 followers and commits at a majority of 5. Why not wait with CompletableFuture.allOf(calls).join() ?","o":["allOf waits for every follower, so one slow or dead follower delays (or blocks) every commit, though only 2 acks are needed","allOf is not thread-safe","allOf returns results in a random order","allOf cannot be combined with timeouts"],"a":0,"w":"Commit latency should be the second-fastest follower here (2 acks plus the leader itself). Waiting for all turns one bad replica into an outage."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A stop flag boolean running is set to false by one thread, but the heartbeat loop in another thread keeps running. What's the fix, and why?","o":["Call System.gc() so the threads synchronise","Make the loop sleep longer between heartbeats","Use an AtomicInteger counter instead of a boolean","Declare it volatile: without a happens-before edge, the compiler may keep reading a cached value forever"],"a":3,"w":"Visibility is only guaranteed through happens-before. A volatile write is seen by later volatile reads; a plain field may never be re-read."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Java Concurrency Refresher","f":"p1-java-concurrency.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the main benefit of having one event-loop thread own all of a node's consensus state?","o":["It uses all CPU cores for protocol logic","State changes are serialized without locks and the node becomes deterministic for a given event order, which also enables replay and simulation testing","Network calls no longer need timeouts","It removes the need to persist the vote to disk"],"a":1,"w":"One owner means no data races on protocol state and a single, replayable order of events. Slow I/O still has to happen on other threads."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A client's call to a payment service times out. What does the client know about the charge?","o":["It failed, so it is safe to tell the user to try again","It succeeded, because the server must have received it","Nothing certain: the charge may have happened, may not have, or may still be in progress","It will be rolled back automatically by the network"],"a":2,"w":"A lost request, a crash before or after the charge, a lost reply and a slow server all look the same from the client: no reply in time."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A dependency's latency is p50 = 30 ms, p99 = 120 ms, p99.9 = 400 ms. You can accept about 0.1% of calls timing out falsely. Which timeout fits?","o":["A little above 400 ms","30 ms, the median","120 ms, the p99","30 seconds, to be safe"],"a":0,"w":"Pick the percentile that matches the false-timeout rate you accept (0.1% means p99.9) and add margin. The p99 would time out 1% of calls that would have succeeded."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A request passes through three services, each with its own 1-second timeout on the next call. What is the problem?","o":["Timeouts don't work across more than two services","The innermost service always times out first","gRPC forbids nested timeouts","The user can wait well over a second, and inner services keep working on requests whose callers have already given up"],"a":3,"w":"Per-hop timeouts don't share a budget. A propagated deadline gives each hop only the time left, so the chain gives up together."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why should a deadline be sent between machines as a remaining duration rather than an absolute wall-clock time?","o":["Durations take fewer bytes","The machines' clocks disagree, so an absolute time from one machine means a different instant on another","Absolute times can't be serialized in Protobuf","The receiving machine has no clock"],"a":1,"w":"Clock skew between machines can be milliseconds or seconds. A remaining duration, measured locally on each side's monotonic clock, avoids it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"RPC & Timeouts","f":"p2-rpc-timeouts.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which reply can a client safely treat as \"definitely not applied\"?","o":["A 504 Gateway Timeout from the load balancer","A connection reset halfway through reading the response","A 400 validation error that the API documents as returned before any work is done","A TimeoutException after 300 ms"],"a":2,"w":"Only an explicit rejection that the server promises happens before acting is a known failure. Gateway errors, resets and timeouts are all unknown outcomes."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In Protobuf, what identifies a field in the encoded bytes?","o":["Its name, written before each value","Its field number, combined with the wire type in a tag","Its position in the message","A hash of the field name"],"a":1,"w":"The tag is (field number << 3) | wire type. Names never appear on the wire, which is why renaming is safe and renumbering is not."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"During a rolling upgrade, a v1 node receives a message from a v2 node that contains a new field. Which property makes this work?","o":["Backward compatibility","Strong typing","Idempotency","Forward compatibility: old code can read data written by new code"],"a":3,"w":"Old reading new is forward compatibility. New reading old (v2 reading v1 data) is backward compatibility. A rolling upgrade needs both."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A team deletes int32 qty = 3 , then later adds int32 discount_pct = 3 . What happens when the new code reads old records?","o":["Old quantities are silently read as discounts, with no error","The parser throws an exception on every old record","The new field reads as 0 for old records","Protobuf detects the reuse at runtime and skips the field"],"a":0,"w":"Same number, same wire type: the bytes are valid, so they are assigned to the new field. Marking 3 as reserved would have prevented the reuse at compile time."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How is the integer 150 encoded as a Protobuf varint?","o":["00 96","96","96 01: low 7 bits first with the continuation bit set, then the remaining bits","01 50"],"a":2,"w":"150 = 1·128 + 22. The first byte carries the low 7 bits (22 = 0x16) with the top bit set: 0x96. The second byte carries 1 without the top bit: 0x01."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Serialization: JSON & Protobuf","f":"p3-serialization.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why do Kafka setups that use Avro usually run a schema registry?","o":["Avro messages are encrypted with the schema","Avro writes no field tags, so a reader needs the writer's exact schema to decode the bytes","Kafka cannot store binary data without it","It converts Avro to JSON for consumers"],"a":1,"w":"Avro values are written in schema order with no tags. The message carries a schema ID; the reader fetches that writer schema and resolves it against its own by field name."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"About how much downtime per year does 99.99% availability allow?","o":["About 8.8 hours","About 53 minutes","About 5 minutes","About 3.65 days"],"a":1,"w":"0.0001 × 365.25 days × 24 h × 60 min ≈ 52.6 minutes. 8.8 hours is three nines; 5 minutes is five nines."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A request must pass through three services in series, each 99.9% available and failing independently. What is the availability of the request path?","o":["99.9%","99.9999999%","About 99.7%","About 97%"],"a":2,"w":"0.999³ ≈ 0.997. Every dependency on the critical path lowers the total."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A component fails on average every 1,000 hours and takes 1 hour to recover. What is its availability, and what is usually the cheaper way to add a nine?","o":["About 99.9%; cut recovery time to about 6 minutes with automatic failover","About 99%; buy hardware that fails ten times less often","About 99.99%; nothing further is needed","About 90%; add more monitoring dashboards"],"a":0,"w":"1000 / 1001 ≈ 99.9%. A tenfold faster recovery (MTTR 6 min) gives about 99.99%, and automation is usually easier than tenfold rarer failures."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does a 4-node consensus cluster tolerate no more failures than a 3-node one?","o":["Even numbers of nodes cannot elect a leader","The fourth node is always a read-only replica","Raft limits clusters to odd sizes","A majority of 4 is 3, so losing 2 nodes already blocks progress, the same as 3 nodes losing 2"],"a":3,"w":"Fault tolerance is n minus the majority: 3 − 2 = 1 and 4 − 3 = 1. Five nodes (majority 3) tolerate two failures."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Failure Arithmetic: Nines & MTBF","f":"p4-failure-arithmetic.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Three database replicas are spread over three racks in one availability zone. Which event does the replica count NOT protect against?","o":["One server's disk failing","A zone-wide power or network failure, or a bad config pushed to all replicas at once","One rack's switch failing","A kernel panic on one server"],"a":1,"w":"Replicas only help against failures they don't share. A zone outage or a fleet-wide config change is a common-mode failure that hits every replica together."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which Java call should a heartbeat-based failure detector use to measure time since the last heartbeat?","o":["System.currentTimeMillis()","Instant.now()","System.nanoTime()","LocalDateTime.now()"],"a":2,"w":"Elapsed time needs the monotonic clock. The other three read the wall clock, which can jump when NTP or an operator changes it."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"NTP finds the local clock 50 ms fast. What does a typical NTP daemon do?","o":["Slews: runs the clock slightly slow until the offset is gone, without any jump","Steps the clock back 50 ms immediately","Reboots the machine","Nothing; 50 ms is within tolerance"],"a":0,"w":"Small offsets are slewed (at most 500 ppm). ntpd steps only above 128 ms by default; chrony steps only when configured to."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did some programs compute negative durations during the leap second of 31 December 2016?","o":["The monotonic clock ran backwards","NTP servers sent invalid packets","Java's Duration class can't represent leap seconds","They subtracted wall-clock readings, and the wall clock repeated a second"],"a":3,"w":"Unix time has no 23:59:60, so most systems repeat 23:59:59. A duration measured across that repeat on the wall clock can come out negative."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How should two System.nanoTime() readings, a and b, be compared to ask \"is b later than a\"?","o":["b > a","b - a > 0","Convert both to Instant first","They can't be compared at all"],"a":1,"w":"nanoTime values can be negative and can wrap, so the Javadoc says to subtract and compare the difference with zero. Readings from different JVMs are not comparable at all."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Clocks on One Machine","f":"p5-clocks-on-one-machine.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A leader holds a lease measured on the wall clock. NTP steps its clock back 2 seconds just before the lease ends. What happens?","o":["The lease ends 2 seconds early","Nothing; leases ignore clock changes","The leader believes its lease is valid for 2 seconds longer than it really is, and may act as leader after a new one exists","The lease is renewed automatically"],"a":2,"w":"Stepping back makes the wall clock read earlier, so the lease's end appears further away. Lease timers belong on the monotonic clock."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A cache cluster using hash mod N grows from 4 to 5 servers. Roughly what fraction of keys now map to a different server?","o":["20%","80%","25%","0%: existing keys stay put"],"a":1,"w":"A key stays only if h mod 4 equals h mod 5, about 1 key in 5. So about 4/5 = 80% move. Consistent hashing would move about 20%."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is Math.abs(hash) % n a bug?","o":["It is slower than floorMod","It gives a different answer on different JVMs","It can't handle n larger than 1,000","Math.abs(Integer.MIN_VALUE) is negative, so a rare key gets a negative shard number"],"a":3,"w":"Integer.MIN_VALUE has no positive counterpart in 32-bit two's complement. Math.floorMod(h, n) always returns a value in [0, n)."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why can't Object.hashCode() be used to choose which server stores a key?","o":["For classes that don't override it, the value is not defined by the object's contents and differs between JVM runs and machines","It returns a 64-bit value","It is too slow","It always returns 0 for strings"],"a":0,"w":"Placement must be computed identically by every client and server. Identity hash codes are per-process. Hash the key's bytes with a fixed function."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With String.hashCode and N = 31 servers, the keys order-000001 to order-001000 land on only 10 servers. Why?","o":["31 is a prime number, which hash tables avoid","The keys are too long for a 32-bit hash","hashCode is a polynomial in 31; the shared prefix adds the same constant to every key, so modulo 31 only the last character varies, and it is one of ten digits","Java caches hash codes for short strings"],"a":2,"w":"The prefix contributes one constant to all the keys, and every other digit term s[i]·31^k with k ≥ 1 is a multiple of 31. So the remainder mod 31 is set by the last character. A well-mixed hash removes such structure."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Hashing for Distribution","f":"p6-hashing.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A perfectly uniform hash spreads keys evenly, yet one server is at 100% CPU while others idle. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The hash function has a bias","A hot key: one key receives a large share of requests, and all of them must go to the server that owns it","The number of servers is even","Clock skew between servers"],"a":1,"w":"Hashing balances the number of keys, not the traffic per key. Hot keys need caching, splitting into sub-keys, or special routing."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What should a first pass over a systems paper cover?","o":["Title, abstract, introduction, headings and conclusion, in about ten minutes","Every proof, to check correctness","The evaluation section only","The full paper, front to back, taking notes on every paragraph"],"a":0,"w":"The first pass is a quick skim to decide whether to read further and to find the category, context, contributions and model."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Raft's safety does not depend on timing, but its availability does. What does that mean?","o":["Raft only works on networks with bounded delays","Raft never needs timeouts","However slow or unpredictable the network is, Raft never commits conflicting entries; it may just fail to make progress until delays settle","Raft's clocks must be synchronised with NTP"],"a":2,"w":"Safety (nothing bad happens) holds under any delays. Liveness (progress) needs delays comfortably below the election timeout."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"\"The system is designed to provide 99.99% availability.\" Which kind of claim is this?","o":["Evidence","Mechanism","Assumption","A goal: it says what the designers aimed for, not that it was achieved"],"a":3,"w":"Look for evidence (measurements over a stated period) before treating a design target as a result."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why read a proof mainly for its statement?","o":["Proofs in systems papers are usually wrong","The statement is the invariant the implementation must preserve, and you can sanity-check it on a small example","Proofs can't be understood without formal training","The statement is shorter"],"a":1,"w":"Knowing the invariant (for example Raft's Leader Completeness) tells you what any change to the code must not break."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"How to Read a Systems Paper","f":"p7-reading-papers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A team builds on the Dynamo paper's design. What is the main risk of relying on the paper alone?","o":["Real systems depart from their papers over time, and the paper says nothing about later changes, defaults, bugs or how the system behaved in production","The Dynamo paper was never peer-reviewed","Papers are copyrighted and can't be implemented","The paper's algorithms are too slow for production"],"a":0,"w":"DynamoDB, for example, differs substantially from the 2007 design. Read documentation, release notes, Jepsen analyses and postmortems as well."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"System Design Building Blocks, Recapped","f":"p8-building-blocks-recap.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which question best separates the easy parts of an architecture from the hard ones?","o":["Which programming language is each service written in?","If this machine vanished, what data would be lost or need to agree with other copies?","How many requests per second does each box handle?","Which boxes run in containers?"],"a":1,"w":"Stateless components can simply be replicated. Components holding state need replication, consensus, partitioning and recovery, which is where distributed systems get hard."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"System Design Building Blocks, Recapped","f":"p8-building-blocks-recap.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A load balancer stops sending traffic to a server that missed three health checks. Which distributed-systems problem is it solving?","o":["Consensus","Clock synchronisation","Partitioning","Failure detection: guessing from missed messages that a node is dead, which may be wrong if it's only slow"],"a":3,"w":"Health checks are heartbeats with a timeout. Chapter 5 covers how to tune them and why false positives are unavoidable."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"System Design Building Blocks, Recapped","f":"p8-building-blocks-recap.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A service inserts an order into its database and then publishes an event, as two separate calls. What failure is NOT handled?","o":["A crash between the two calls loses the event even though the order exists","A slow database query","A malformed order request","A full disk on the client"],"a":0,"w":"Two independent writes aren't atomic. A transactional outbox writes the event in the same transaction as the order and publishes it afterwards."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"System Design Building Blocks, Recapped","f":"p8-building-blocks-recap.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In GitHub's October 2018 incident, why did a 43-second network partition cause about 24 hours of degraded service?","o":["The data centre lost power for a day","Caches had to be rebuilt from scratch","Automatic failover promoted databases in another region, and both sides accepted writes that then had to be reconciled","DNS records took 24 hours to expire"],"a":2,"w":"The partition was short; the failover it triggered created divergent writes on two sides, and reconciling them safely took most of a day."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"System Design Building Blocks, Recapped","f":"p8-building-blocks-recap.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The etcd cluster behind a Kubernetes control plane loses its majority. What is the most likely effect?","o":["All running containers stop immediately","Already-running workloads keep running, but nothing that needs to write cluster state (new deployments, rescheduling, leader changes) can make progress","etcd switches to eventual consistency","Kubernetes elects a new etcd from the worker nodes"],"a":1,"w":"Without a majority, etcd can't commit writes. Existing pods keep running, but the control plane is frozen. Chapter 44 looks at this in detail."},{"n":1,"ti":"Why Distributed Is Hard: The Eight Fallacies","f":"01-why-distributed-is-hard.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does \"partial failure\" mean?","o":["A request that returns only some of the requested data","A disk that has some bad sectors","Some components fail while others keep running, and the survivors may not be able to tell which failed","A deployment that reaches only part of the fleet"],"a":2,"w":"On one machine, a crash takes everything down together. In a distributed system, parts fail independently, and from outside a crashed node looks like a slow one."},{"n":1,"ti":"Why Distributed Is Hard: The Eight Fallacies","f":"01-why-distributed-is-hard.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A client adds retries to calls that sometimes time out, but no idempotency key. Under packet loss, what new outcome appears?","o":["Duplicate side effects: a request whose reply was lost is applied again by the retry","Requests are applied zero times","Threads get stuck forever","Nothing new: retries only help"],"a":0,"w":"A timeout often means \"applied, but reply lost\". Retrying such a request applies it twice unless the server can recognise the retry."},{"n":1,"ti":"Why Distributed Is Hard: The Eight Fallacies","f":"01-why-distributed-is-hard.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"After a database failover, some application servers keep connecting to the old database IP until they are restarted. Which fallacy is this?","o":["Bandwidth is infinite","The network is secure","Transport cost is zero","Topology doesn't change"],"a":3,"w":"The client assumed the address behind a name never changes, typically by caching DNS forever. Addresses and membership need expiry and refresh."},{"n":1,"ti":"Why Distributed Is Hard: The Eight Fallacies","f":"01-why-distributed-is-hard.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A client with no timeout sends a request, and the request packet is lost. What happens to the calling thread?","o":["The operating system retries the request automatically until it succeeds","It may wait indefinitely, holding its resources, because no reply will ever arrive","It receives an error immediately","The server notices and sends an error"],"a":1,"w":"The server never saw the request, so nothing will ever reply. Without a timeout (and, with TCP, without keepalives), the caller can wait forever."},{"n":1,"ti":"Why Distributed Is Hard: The Eight Fallacies","f":"01-why-distributed-is-hard.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can't a distributed system order events from different machines just by their timestamps?","o":["Timestamps take too many bytes to send","Java doesn't support nanosecond timestamps","There is no global clock: each machine's clock is slightly different, so timestamps from two machines can be in the wrong order","Events on different machines never need ordering"],"a":2,"w":"Clock skew between machines can exceed the time between events. Logical clocks (Chapters 7–8) order events without relying on physical time."},{"n":2,"ti":"System Models","f":"02-system-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In which timing model can a node be certain that a silent peer has crashed?","o":["Asynchronous","Synchronous, where message delays and processing times have known bounds","Partially synchronous","None of them"],"a":1,"w":"Only known bounds let silence prove a crash. In the other two models, a silent node might just be slow (or, under partial synchrony, the bound may not hold yet)."},{"n":2,"ti":"System Models","f":"02-system-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does the crash-recovery model require of a node that replies \"vote granted\"?","o":["Nothing; votes are only kept in memory","It must reply within a fixed time bound","It must sign the reply cryptographically","It must have written the vote to stable storage before replying, so a restart can't make it vote again"],"a":3,"w":"Crash-recovery nodes lose memory on restart but keep what was fsync'd. Promises to other nodes must be durable before they are sent."},{"n":2,"ti":"System Models","f":"02-system-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Under partial synchrony with crash failures, how many failed nodes can a 5-node consensus cluster tolerate?","o":["2: a majority of 3 must remain","4: any one survivor is enough","1","0"],"a":0,"w":"Crash-tolerant consensus without a perfect failure detector needs f < n/2. With n = 5, f = 2."},{"n":2,"ti":"System Models","f":"02-system-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How do Raft and Paxos stay safe even though their timeouts can be wrong?","o":["They use synchronised clocks","They retry until the timeout is right","Timeouts only trigger elections (liveness); safety comes from majorities and terms, which don't depend on timing","They assume a synchronous network"],"a":2,"w":"A wrong timeout can cause an unnecessary election and a pause, never two conflicting committed values."},{"n":2,"ti":"System Models","f":"02-system-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which failure model allows a node to send different, contradictory messages to different peers?","o":["Crash-stop","Byzantine","Crash-recovery","Omission"],"a":1,"w":"Byzantine nodes can behave arbitrarily, including equivocating. Tolerating f of them needs at least 3f + 1 nodes."},{"n":3,"ti":"RPC in Java with gRPC","f":"03-grpc-java.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A gRPC call returns DEADLINE_EXCEEDED. What does the client know?","o":["The server rejected the request","The request never reached the server","The server completed the request successfully","Nothing certain about whether the work was applied; retry only if the call is idempotent"],"a":3,"w":"The deadline passed before a reply arrived. The server may have finished, partly finished, or never started."},{"n":3,"ti":"RPC in Java with gRPC","f":"03-grpc-java.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"An order service handler calls inventory through a blocking stub, without setting a deadline on it. The incoming call had 300 ms left. What deadline does the inventory call get in gRPC Java?","o":["None: it waits forever","The remaining time of the incoming call, inherited from the current Context","A default of 30 seconds","Exactly 300 ms, reset at the start of the call"],"a":1,"w":"The incoming deadline lives in the Context, and outgoing calls made from that Context inherit what is left of it, unless the work escapes the Context, for example onto an unwrapped executor."},{"n":3,"ti":"RPC in Java with gRPC","f":"03-grpc-java.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why should a gRPC client create one channel per target and reuse it?","o":["A channel holds connections, name resolution and load balancing state; creating one per call adds handshakes and defeats balancing","gRPC allows only one channel per JVM","Channels can't be closed","Each channel uses a separate thread per call"],"a":0,"w":"Channels are designed to be long-lived and shared; they multiplex many concurrent calls over HTTP/2."},{"n":3,"ti":"RPC in Java with gRPC","f":"03-grpc-java.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A gRPC service is scaled from 3 to 10 pods behind a layer-4 load balancer, but the new pods receive almost no requests. Why?","o":["The new pods failed their health checks","gRPC only supports three servers","Clients keep their existing long-lived HTTP/2 connections, and a layer-4 balancer only balances new connections","Protobuf messages are too small to balance"],"a":2,"w":"All calls are multiplexed over connections opened before scaling. Balance per request: client-side round robin with a headless service, or a layer-7 proxy."},{"n":3,"ti":"RPC in Java with gRPC","f":"03-grpc-java.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which status code should a server return when a client sends a negative quantity?","o":["UNAVAILABLE","INVALID_ARGUMENT, so the client knows nothing was done and retrying won't help","INTERNAL","DEADLINE_EXCEEDED"],"a":1,"w":"INVALID_ARGUMENT means the request itself is wrong regardless of system state. Retrying it is pointless; the client must fix it."},{"n":4,"ti":"Retries, Backoff & Idempotency","f":"04-retries-idempotency.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A browser, an API gateway and a backend service each retry a failed call up to 3 times (4 attempts each). How many database calls can one click cause in the worst case?","o":["4","12","64","9"],"a":2,"w":"Attempts multiply across layers: 4 × 4 × 4 = 64. Retry at one layer and fail fast elsewhere."},{"n":4,"ti":"Retries, Backoff & Idempotency","f":"04-retries-idempotency.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does full jitter compute for the wait before retry number n?","o":["A random value between 0 and min(cap, base × 2ⁿ)","Exactly base × 2ⁿ","base × 2ⁿ plus or minus 1 ms","A fixed 100 ms"],"a":0,"w":"Full jitter picks uniformly from zero up to the capped exponential value, which spreads synchronized clients evenly."},{"n":4,"ti":"Retries, Backoff & Idempotency","f":"04-retries-idempotency.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A service recovers from a 3-second outage, but stays overloaded for an hour while clients keep retrying with backoff and jitter. What would most directly let it recover?","o":["Increasing the clients' timeout to 60 seconds","Switching from full jitter to equal jitter","Adding more retry attempts","A retry budget that caps retries to a fraction of successes, plus a server that drops requests whose deadline has passed"],"a":3,"w":"This is a metastable failure: retries keep the load above capacity. Capping total retries and not wasting work on abandoned requests break the loop."},{"n":4,"ti":"Retries, Backoff & Idempotency","f":"04-retries-idempotency.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"When should a client generate the idempotency key for a payment?","o":["Fresh for each attempt, so the server can tell attempts apart","Once per logical payment, before the first attempt, and reuse it for every retry","After the first timeout","The server should generate it"],"a":1,"w":"The key identifies the logical operation. A new key per attempt makes every retry look like a new payment."},{"n":4,"ti":"Retries, Backoff & Idempotency","f":"04-retries-idempotency.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two retries with the same idempotency key arrive at the server at the same moment. What should happen?","o":["Both are processed, and the second result is stored","Both are rejected","An atomic claim lets exactly one proceed; the other is told the request is in progress (or waits for its result)","The one with the earlier timestamp wins"],"a":2,"w":"The claim must be atomic, for example a unique index or conditional insert, so concurrent duplicates can't both act."},{"n":5,"ti":"Failure Detectors & Heartbeats","f":"05-failure-detectors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does \"completeness\" mean for a failure detector?","o":["It never suspects a live node","Every crashed node is eventually suspected","It monitors every node in the cluster","It detects crashes within one heartbeat interval"],"a":1,"w":"Completeness is about catching real failures; accuracy is about not suspecting live nodes. No detector achieves both perfectly in an asynchronous network."},{"n":5,"ti":"Failure Detectors & Heartbeats","f":"05-failure-detectors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A phi accrual detector reports φ = 8 for a node. What does that mean?","o":["The node has missed 8 heartbeats","The node has been silent for 8 seconds","Given recent heartbeat timing, the chance a healthy node would be this late is about 1 in 10⁸","8 other nodes agree that it is dead"],"a":2,"w":"φ is minus log10 of the probability that a heartbeat would arrive this late if the node were fine. Higher φ means stronger suspicion."},{"n":5,"ti":"Failure Detectors & Heartbeats","f":"05-failure-detectors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does SWIM send ping-req messages through other members before suspecting a node?","o":["So that one bad link between the prober and the target doesn't make a live node look dead","To measure the network's bandwidth","To elect a leader for the probe","To reduce the number of messages to zero"],"a":0,"w":"Indirect probes test other network paths. A target is suspected only if none of them reach it."},{"n":5,"ti":"Failure Detectors & Heartbeats","f":"05-failure-detectors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On a jittery cross-region network, a fixed 1.5 s timeout flags healthy nodes several times an hour. What does phi accrual do differently?","o":["It ignores the network and uses CPU load instead","It always waits exactly twice as long","It requires a majority vote before suspecting","It learns the wider spread of heartbeat intervals, so φ rises more slowly and the same threshold tolerates more delay"],"a":3,"w":"The detector models recent inter-arrival times. A wider distribution means a late heartbeat is less surprising, so suspicion grows more slowly."},{"n":5,"ti":"Failure Detectors & Heartbeats","f":"05-failure-detectors.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is a good response to a node being suspected by a failure detector?","o":["Immediately delete its data and re-replicate everything it held","Take cheap, reversible actions first (route around it), and save irreversible ones for stronger evidence such as an expired lease","Restart every other node to be safe","Ignore it until a human confirms"],"a":1,"w":"Suspicions are sometimes wrong. Designing so that a false positive costs little is the main defence."},{"n":6,"ti":"Physical Clocks, NTP & Skew","f":"06-ntp-clock-skew.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A server's clock drifts at 50 ppm and its time daemon has been stopped for a day. Roughly how far off could it be?","o":["50 microseconds","50 milliseconds","About 4 seconds","About 50 seconds"],"a":2,"w":"50 microseconds for each of 86,400 seconds is 4.3 seconds."},{"n":6,"ti":"Physical Clocks, NTP & Skew","f":"06-ntp-clock-skew.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In an NTP exchange the request takes 60 ms and the reply takes 20 ms. How wrong is the offset estimate?","o":["20 ms: half the difference between the two directions","40 ms: the whole difference","80 ms: the round-trip delay","It is exact"],"a":0,"w":"The calculation assumes equal delays. An asymmetry of 40 ms shifts the estimate by 20 ms, and the client can't tell."},{"n":6,"ti":"Physical Clocks, NTP & Skew","f":"06-ntp-clock-skew.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What can an NTP client guarantee about its offset estimate from one exchange?","o":["That it is exact","That it is within 1 ms","Nothing at all","That its error is no more than half the measured round-trip delay"],"a":3,"w":"At worst one direction took the whole round trip. That is the only bound the timestamps support."},{"n":6,"ti":"Physical Clocks, NTP & Skew","f":"06-ntp-clock-skew.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two replicas use last-write-wins. Node A's clock is 100 ms fast. A write goes to A, and 30 ms later a newer write to the same key goes to B. What is stored?","o":["The newer write, since it arrived later","The older write: its timestamp from A's fast clock is larger, so the newer one is discarded","Both, as siblings","An error is returned to the second client"],"a":1,"w":"\"Last\" means the largest timestamp, and A's are 100 ms ahead. The newer write is dropped with no error."},{"n":6,"ti":"Physical Clocks, NTP & Skew","f":"06-ntp-clock-skew.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does PTP do that NTP doesn't?","o":["It uses atomic clocks where NTP uses GPS","It works over the public internet","It timestamps packets in network hardware, and has switches account for the delay they add","It removes clock drift"],"a":2,"w":"Hardware timestamps remove software jitter, and switches that report their own delay remove most of the path uncertainty."},{"n":7,"ti":"Lamport Clocks & Happens-Before","f":"07-lamport-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two events are \"concurrent\" in Lamport's sense when:","o":["They happened at exactly the same physical time","Neither could have influenced the other: no chain of local steps and messages leads from one to the other","They have equal Lamport timestamps","They happened at the same process"],"a":1,"w":"Concurrency is about causality. Two events at one process are always ordered, and concurrent events can be hours apart."},{"n":7,"ti":"Lamport Clocks & Happens-Before","f":"07-lamport-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A process whose clock reads 4 receives a message stamped 9. What is its clock after the receipt?","o":["5","9","13","10"],"a":3,"w":"max(4, 9) + 1. The receipt must have a larger timestamp than the send that caused it."},{"n":7,"ti":"Lamport Clocks & Happens-Before","f":"07-lamport-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Event a has Lamport timestamp 3 and event b has 8, on different processes. What can you conclude?","o":["Only that b did not happen before a","That a happened before b","That a and b are concurrent","That a took place five steps earlier in real time"],"a":0,"w":"The clock condition says causes have smaller timestamps. It doesn't say that smaller timestamps are causes."},{"n":7,"ti":"Lamport Clocks & Happens-Before","f":"07-lamport-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is a tie-break by process ID added to Lamport timestamps?","o":["To make the timestamps reflect real time","To detect concurrent events","To make the order total, so that every process sorts all events the same way","To make the clocks tick faster"],"a":2,"w":"Equal timestamps would otherwise be unordered. The tie-break is arbitrary, and its value is that everyone applies the same one."},{"n":7,"ti":"Lamport Clocks & Happens-Before","f":"07-lamport-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A Raft node in term 5 receives a message from a node in term 7. Following the Lamport rule, what does it do?","o":["Rejects the message as invalid","Moves to term 7, and steps down if it thought it was leader","Replies with term 6","Waits for a majority to confirm the term"],"a":1,"w":"Terms act as a logical clock: on receipt, take the maximum. A larger term shows the node's own view is out of date."},{"n":8,"ti":"Vector Clocks","f":"08-vector-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How are the vectors [3, 1, 2] and [2, 4, 2] related?","o":["The first is before the second","The second is before the first","They are concurrent","They are equal"],"a":2,"w":"The first is ahead in entry one and the second in entry two. Neither has seen everything the other has."},{"n":8,"ti":"Vector Clocks","f":"08-vector-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Process P2 has vector [1, 2, 0] and receives a message carrying [3, 0, 1]. What is its vector afterwards?","o":["[3, 3, 1]","[3, 2, 1]","[4, 2, 1]","[4, 3, 2]"],"a":0,"w":"Entry-wise maximum gives [3, 2, 1], and then P2 adds one to its own entry."},{"n":8,"ti":"Vector Clocks","f":"08-vector-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A store finds two versions of a key whose version vectors are concurrent. What should it do?","o":["Keep the one with the larger sum of entries","Keep the one that arrived most recently","Reject both and return an error","Keep both as siblings, and return both to the next reader to merge"],"a":3,"w":"Concurrent means neither writer saw the other's value. Choosing one in the store discards an update nobody chose to discard."},{"n":8,"ti":"Vector Clocks","f":"08-vector-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why do Dynamo-style stores key their vectors by server and not by client?","o":["Clients can't be trusted to count","There are far more clients than servers, and the vector has an entry for everything that has ever updated the item","Server IDs sort faster","Clients don't have clocks"],"a":1,"w":"Vector size grows with the number of updaters. A handful of replicas keeps it small."},{"n":8,"ti":"Vector Clocks","f":"08-vector-clocks.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does a hybrid logical clock give up, compared with a vector clock?","o":["Causes sorting before effects","A fixed size","The ability to tell that two events were concurrent","Any connection to wall-clock time"],"a":2,"w":"It is a Lamport clock kept close to physical time: compact and causal, and unable to detect conflicts."},{"n":9,"ti":"Replication Basics","f":"09-replication-basics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A user updates their profile, and the next page shows the old profile. Replication is single-leader and asynchronous. What happened?","o":["The write was lost","The read was served by a follower that hadn't yet received the write","Two leaders conflicted","The leader rejected the write"],"a":1,"w":"Replication lag. The follower will catch up. Reading your own writes from the leader avoids it."},{"n":9,"ti":"Replication Basics","f":"09-replication-basics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which problem exists with multi-leader replication and not with a single leader?","o":["Replication lag","Stale reads","The need for failover","Two replicas accepting conflicting writes to the same record"],"a":3,"w":"One leader puts every write in a single order. Several leaders can each accept a write the others haven't seen."},{"n":9,"ti":"Replication Basics","f":"09-replication-basics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A leader acknowledges a write, then crashes before any follower has received it. Under which mode is the write lost?","o":["Asynchronous","Wait for one follower","Wait for a majority","None: an acknowledged write is never lost"],"a":0,"w":"Asynchronous replication acknowledges when only the leader has the write. The other modes couldn't have acknowledged it yet."},{"n":9,"ti":"Replication Basics","f":"09-replication-basics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is fully synchronous replication, waiting for every replica, rarely used?","o":["It loses data","It causes write conflicts","One slow or failed replica stops all writes","It needs synchronised clocks"],"a":2,"w":"Availability falls to that of the least available replica. Waiting for some of them keeps durability without that."},{"n":9,"ti":"Replication Basics","f":"09-replication-basics.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In a leaderless system with three replicas, one replica is down. What happens to a write that needs two acknowledgements?","o":["It fails until a new leader is elected","It succeeds on the other two, and the missing replica is brought up to date later","It is queued until the replica returns","It succeeds, and the down replica's data is discarded"],"a":1,"w":"There is no leader to fail over. Two acknowledgements are enough, and repair happens afterwards."},{"n":10,"ti":"CAP & PACELC","f":"10-cap-pacelc.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is \"pick any two of C, A and P\" a misleading summary of CAP?","o":["Because all three can be had with enough hardware","Because the theorem has since been disproved","Because partitions can't be opted out of, so the real choice is between C and A, and only while a partition lasts","Because it applies only to NoSQL databases"],"a":2,"w":"A replicated system will see partitions. \"CA\" just means the choice hasn't been thought about."},{"n":10,"ti":"CAP & PACELC","f":"10-cap-pacelc.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"During a partition, a five-node CP system is split three and two. What happens?","o":["The side with three carries on, and the side with two refuses requests","Both sides refuse all requests","Both sides carry on and merge later","The side with two takes over"],"a":0,"w":"Only the majority can be sure of holding the latest data. The minority gives up availability to stay correct."},{"n":10,"ti":"CAP & PACELC","f":"10-cap-pacelc.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does the \"ELC\" part of PACELC describe?","o":["What happens when a leader is elected","The behaviour during a partition","Eventual, linearizable and causal consistency","With no partition, the trade-off between latency and consistency"],"a":3,"w":"Else: Latency or Consistency. Waiting for other replicas costs time on every request, partition or not."},{"n":10,"ti":"CAP & PACELC","f":"10-cap-pacelc.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A store answers every read from the nearest replica without checking the others, and keeps accepting writes on both sides of a partition. How is it classified?","o":["PC/EC","PA/EL","PC/EL","PA/EC"],"a":1,"w":"Availability during a partition, and latency otherwise: the Dynamo-style default."},{"n":10,"ti":"CAP & PACELC","f":"10-cap-pacelc.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In CAP, what does \"availability\" require?","o":["That the system is up at least 99.9% of the time","That a majority of nodes respond","That every node which hasn't failed responds to every request without an error","That reads are fast"],"a":2,"w":"The theorem's definition is strict. A CP system can have excellent uptime and still not be \"available\" in this sense."},{"n":11,"ti":"Consistency Models","f":"11-consistency-models.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A write of x = 1 completes. A read that starts afterwards returns the old value, 0. Which is the strongest model this history can still satisfy?","o":["Linearizability","Sequential consistency","None of them","Only eventual consistency"],"a":1,"w":"Ordering the read before the write explains the values, and the reader was a different client. Real time is violated, so it isn't linearizable."},{"n":11,"ti":"Consistency Models","f":"11-consistency-models.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does linearizability require that sequential consistency doesn't?","o":["That each client's own operations stay in order","That all replicas eventually converge","That transactions are isolated","That the single order agrees with real time: an operation that finished before another began comes first"],"a":3,"w":"Both demand one order. Linearizability also ties it to the clock."},{"n":11,"ti":"Consistency Models","f":"11-consistency-models.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Two unrelated comments are posted at the same moment, and two readers see them in opposite orders. Which model does that violate?","o":["Sequential consistency, and so linearizability. Causal consistency allows it","Causal consistency","Monotonic reads","Eventual consistency"],"a":0,"w":"No single order suits both readers. The comments are concurrent, so no cause was shown after its effect."},{"n":11,"ti":"Consistency Models","f":"11-consistency-models.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A user reads a comment, refreshes, and the comment is gone, then reappears on the next refresh. Which guarantee was broken?","o":["Read your writes","Monotonic writes","Monotonic reads","Eventual consistency"],"a":2,"w":"The second read came from a replica further behind than the first. A client should never see an older state after a newer one."},{"n":11,"ti":"Consistency Models","f":"11-consistency-models.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does eventual consistency guarantee about a read made one second after a write?","o":["That it returns the new value","Nothing","That it returns the new value if it goes to the same replica","That it returns either the old or the new value, and never anything else"],"a":1,"w":"The promise is convergence at some later time if writes stop. No particular read is covered."},{"n":12,"ti":"Quorums","f":"12-quorums.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"N = 5. Which setting guarantees that a read sees the latest completed write?","o":["W = 2, R = 2","W = 3, R = 2","W = 3, R = 3","W = 1, R = 4"],"a":2,"w":"3 + 3 = 6 exceeds 5. The others sum to 5 or less, so a read set can avoid the write set."},{"n":12,"ti":"Quorums","f":"12-quorums.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"N = 3, W = 2, R = 2. How many replicas can be down with both reads and writes still succeeding?","o":["One","Two","None","Three"],"a":0,"w":"Each operation needs two of the three. With two down, neither quorum can be reached."},{"n":12,"ti":"Quorums","f":"12-quorums.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does a sloppy quorum give up?","o":["Durability","Write availability","The ability to use hints","The guarantee that the read set and the write set overlap, since stand-in nodes count towards W"],"a":3,"w":"The data is on W machines, and they may not be the key's home replicas, which is where reads look."},{"n":12,"ti":"Quorums","f":"12-quorums.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why isn't read repair enough to keep replicas in step?","o":["It is too slow","It only repairs keys that are read, so data that is rarely read stays under-replicated","It can't compare versions","It works only with sloppy quorums"],"a":1,"w":"A background anti-entropy process is needed for everything that reads don't touch."},{"n":12,"ti":"Quorums","f":"12-quorums.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"With strict quorums and R + W > N, which of these can still happen?","o":["A read that starts after a write has completed misses it","The read set and the write set are disjoint","While a write is in progress, one read returns the new value and a later read returns the old one","A write succeeds with fewer than W acknowledgements"],"a":2,"w":"The rule covers completed writes. During a write, readers can see it or not, in either order, which linearizability forbids."},{"n":13,"ti":"Leader Election","f":"13-leader-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In the bully algorithm, node 3 of nodes 1 to 6 notices that the leader, node 6, has died. What does it do first?","o":["Declares itself leader","Sends ELECTION to nodes 1 and 2","Sends ELECTION to nodes 4, 5 and 6","Waits for node 5 to announce itself"],"a":2,"w":"It challenges every node with a higher number. It wins only if none of them answers."},{"n":13,"ti":"Leader Election","f":"13-leader-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Five nodes are split into groups of two and three by a partition. The leader is in the group of three. With the plain bully algorithm, what happens?","o":["The group of two elects its own leader, and there are two leaders","The group of two waits for the network to heal","Both groups lose their leader","The original leader steps down"],"a":0,"w":"The two nodes can't tell a partition from a crash, and the algorithm has no rule against a minority electing."},{"n":13,"ti":"Leader Election","f":"13-leader-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does requiring a majority to win an election prevent two leaders being elected during a partition?","o":["A majority is faster to contact","The minority side shuts down","Majorities always include the old leader","Any two majorities share a node, so two disconnected sides can't both have one"],"a":3,"w":"There aren't enough nodes for two disjoint majorities. At most one side can elect."},{"n":13,"ti":"Leader Election","f":"13-leader-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A leader holds a 10-second lease. The network to the lease store fails. What must the leader do?","o":["Carry on as leader until the network returns","Stop acting as leader before the lease expires, without being told","Extend its own lease locally","Hold an election among the nodes it can reach"],"a":1,"w":"The lease is the leader's promise to stop. The others will elect a successor once it has run out."},{"n":13,"ti":"Leader Election","f":"13-leader-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A leader confirms its lease is valid, then pauses for 30 seconds in garbage collection, then performs a write. What stops that write doing damage?","o":["The lease, which the leader checked","The majority rule","A fencing token: the resource rejects requests carrying an older term than it has already seen","Nothing can"],"a":2,"w":"The leader can't see its own pause. Only the receiving end can tell that the request comes from a superseded leader."},{"n":14,"ti":"Primary–Backup Replication","f":"14-primary-backup.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does the primary wait for the backup's acknowledgement before replying to the client?","o":["To balance the load between the two servers","So that every operation a client is told about exists on both servers, and survives the loss of either","Because the backup computes the result","To give the view service time to check its pings"],"a":1,"w":"A reply is a promise. Without the wait, a crash of the primary right after replying would break it."},{"n":14,"ti":"Primary–Backup Replication","f":"14-primary-backup.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"The primary and the backup both fail, and an idle server is healthy. What should the view service do?","o":["Promote the idle server to primary","Promote the idle server to backup","Hold an election among the clients","Nothing, until a server holding the state returns"],"a":3,"w":"The idle server has no data. Promoting it would replace the service's contents with an empty state."},{"n":14,"ti":"Primary–Backup Replication","f":"14-primary-backup.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why must the view service wait for the primary to acknowledge the current view before moving to the next?","o":["Otherwise it could promote a backup that hasn't finished receiving the state","To keep the view numbers consecutive","Because clients cache the view","To avoid sending too many messages"],"a":0,"w":"The acknowledgement says \"my backup is ready\". Without it, a chain of quick view changes could end at an empty server."},{"n":14,"ti":"Primary–Backup Replication","f":"14-primary-backup.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A primary that has been replaced, without knowing it, receives a client's write. What happens?","o":["It applies the write, and the two primaries diverge","It ignores the write","It forwards the write to its former backup, which rejects the old view number, so the write can't complete","The view service intercepts the request"],"a":2,"w":"A primary never acts alone, and the server it depends on already knows about the new view."},{"n":14,"ti":"Primary–Backup Replication","f":"14-primary-backup.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In chain replication, why can the tail answer reads without consulting any other server?","o":["It holds a lease","A write reaches the tail only after every other server has applied it, so whatever the tail has is on all of them","It forwards each read to the head first","Reads don't need to be consistent"],"a":1,"w":"The tail is the last to see each write, so it never returns anything that a failure could take away."},{"n":15,"ti":"State Machine Replication","f":"15-state-machine-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which three conditions make replicas of a state machine stay identical?","o":["The same hardware, the same clock and the same network","The same leader, the same term and the same majority","The same initial state, the same deterministic commands, and the same order","The same commands, applied at the same wall-clock time, by the same thread"],"a":2,"w":"Nothing else is needed, and losing any one of the three lets the copies diverge."},{"n":15,"ti":"State Machine Replication","f":"15-state-machine-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A command sets a key to expire \"in 60 seconds\". How should a replicated state machine handle it?","o":["The leader computes the absolute expiry time once, and that time is stored in the log entry","Each replica adds 60 seconds to its own clock when it applies the entry","Each replica starts a 60-second timer","Expiry can't be supported"],"a":0,"w":"Replicas apply the entry at different moments, on different clocks. The value has to be decided once and carried in the command."},{"n":15,"ti":"State Machine Replication","f":"15-state-machine-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why isn't it enough for replicas to agree on the set of commands?","o":["Sets can't be sent over a network","Some commands might be duplicated","A set takes more memory than a log","Most commands don't commute, so the same commands in different orders give different states"],"a":3,"w":"\"Add 50, then double\" and \"double, then add 50\" start and end in different places."},{"n":15,"ti":"State Machine Replication","f":"15-state-machine-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is an entry applied to the state machine only after it has been committed?","o":["To give followers time to catch up","An uncommitted entry may still be replaced, and the state machine has no way to undo an apply","Because applying is slow","To keep the log short"],"a":1,"w":"Commitment is the promise that the entry will stay at that index. Before it, the result could turn out to belong to a history that never happened."},{"n":15,"ti":"State Machine Replication","f":"15-state-machine-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A snapshot is taken after applying entry 5,000. What must it record besides the data?","o":["The wall-clock time it was taken","The names of the replicas","That it covers the log up to entry 5,000, together with any other state apply reads, such as the client de-duplication table","Nothing else"],"a":2,"w":"Replay must resume at exactly entry 5,001, with everything in place that later commands depend on."},{"n":16,"ti":"Paxos","f":"16-paxos.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"An acceptor has promised proposal number 7. It receives Prepare(5). What does it do?","o":["Promises 5 as well","Refuses, since it has promised a higher number","Forgets 7 and promises 5","Accepts the value from proposal 5"],"a":1,"w":"A promise for 7 is a promise to ignore everything lower. The proposer learns that it is out of date and must use a higher number."},{"n":16,"ti":"Paxos","f":"16-paxos.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A proposer wanting value B receives three promises. Two report nothing accepted, and one reports the accepted proposal (4, \"A\"). What must it send in phase 2?","o":["Its own value, B, since most acceptors had nothing","Nothing: it must abandon the attempt","Both A and B","The value A, under its own proposal number"],"a":3,"w":"A might already be chosen by acceptors it didn't hear from. It has to carry A forward."},{"n":16,"ti":"Paxos","f":"16-paxos.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"When is a value \"chosen\" in Paxos?","o":["When a majority of acceptors have accepted the same numbered proposal","When a proposer receives a majority of promises","When any acceptor accepts it","When all acceptors have accepted it"],"a":0,"w":"Promises grant the right to propose. Only acceptances by a majority, for one proposal, choose."},{"n":16,"ti":"Paxos","f":"16-paxos.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Two proposers keep sending prepares with ever higher numbers, each causing the other's accept to be refused. Which property is being violated?","o":["Agreement","Validity","Termination: nothing wrong is chosen, and nothing is chosen at all","None: this can't happen"],"a":2,"w":"Paxos stays safe and stops making progress. A random back-off, or a single leader, restores it."},{"n":16,"ti":"Paxos","f":"16-paxos.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does Multi-Paxos save, compared with running basic Paxos for every log entry?","o":["The need for majorities","Phase 1 for each entry: a stable leader runs it once, then needs only one round trip for each command","The need to write to disk","The acceptors"],"a":1,"w":"Phase 1 carries no value, so one prepare can cover every later position, until the leader changes."},{"n":17,"ti":"Raft I: Terms & Leader Election","f":"17-raft-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A leader in term 4 receives a message carrying term 6. What does it do?","o":["Rejects it, since it is the leader","Starts an election for term 7","Sets its term to 6 and becomes a follower","Keeps leading, and raises its term to 6"],"a":2,"w":"A higher term means the cluster has moved on. Every server yields to it, whatever its role."},{"n":17,"ti":"Raft I: Terms & Leader Election","f":"17-raft-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why can't two candidates both win the election for the same term?","o":["Each server votes for one candidate in a term, and two majorities would need some server to have voted twice","The candidate with the higher ID always wins","Candidates check with each other before claiming victory","Only one server's timer can run out"],"a":0,"w":"A majority for each would overlap in at least one server, which had only one vote to give."},{"n":17,"ti":"Raft I: Terms & Leader Election","f":"17-raft-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What goes wrong if every server uses the same fixed election timeout?","o":["Two leaders are elected","The term number stops increasing","Heartbeats stop working","Servers time out together, each votes for itself, and elections keep ending with no winner"],"a":3,"w":"Safety holds: nobody wins wrongly. Nobody wins at all, since the votes always split."},{"n":17,"ti":"Raft I: Terms & Leader Election","f":"17-raft-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Which two things must a Raft server write to disk before answering a RequestVote?","o":["Its role and its election timeout","Its current term and the candidate it voted for","The leader's ID and the time of the last heartbeat","Nothing: votes are kept in memory"],"a":1,"w":"Otherwise a crash and restart would let it vote again in the same term."},{"n":17,"ti":"Raft I: Terms & Leader Election","f":"17-raft-election.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Five servers are split two and three by a partition. What happens on the side with two?","o":["They elect a leader between them","They shut down","They stand for election repeatedly and never win, since two votes aren't a majority of five","They follow the old leader from memory"],"a":2,"w":"A majority is counted over the whole cluster. The minority side can't elect, and so can't diverge."},{"n":18,"ti":"Raft II: Log Replication & Safety","f":"18-raft-log-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A follower receives AppendEntries with prevLogIndex 6 and prevLogTerm 3. Its own entry 6 is from term 2. What does it do?","o":["Appends the new entries after its entry 6","Refuses, and the leader retries from an earlier index","Deletes its whole log","Starts an election"],"a":1,"w":"The logs don't join at 6. The leader backs up until it finds a matching entry, and replaces everything after it."},{"n":18,"ti":"Raft II: Log Replication & Safety","f":"18-raft-log-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Two servers each have an entry at index 8 from term 4. What does the log matching property say?","o":["Only that entry 8 holds the same command on both","That both entries are committed","That both servers are in term 4","That their logs are identical from entry 1 to entry 8"],"a":3,"w":"Each entry was only appended after its predecessor was checked, so agreement at 8 implies agreement all the way back."},{"n":18,"ti":"Raft II: Log Replication & Safety","f":"18-raft-log-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A candidate's log ends at index 9, term 2. A voter's log ends at index 4, term 3. Does the voter grant its vote?","o":["No: the voter's last entry is from a later term, so its log is more up to date","Yes: the candidate's log is longer","Yes: every server votes for the first candidate to ask","No: the logs are different lengths"],"a":0,"w":"The last term is compared first, and length only breaks ties."},{"n":18,"ti":"Raft II: Log Replication & Safety","f":"18-raft-log-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A new leader in term 5 finds an entry from term 3 in its log, and gets it on to a majority. When may it treat that entry as committed?","o":["At once: it is on a majority","Never: old entries are discarded","Once an entry from term 5 is committed, which commits everything before it","After every server has it"],"a":2,"w":"An old-term entry on a majority can still lose to a later-term entry elsewhere. A committed entry of the current term rules that out."},{"n":18,"ti":"Raft II: Log Replication & Safety","f":"18-raft-log-replication.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why can't a cluster switch from three members to five in a single step?","o":["The log would be too long","While some servers use the old list and some the new, a majority of each can exist with no server in common","Terms can't change during a membership change","Five servers can't form a majority"],"a":1,"w":"Two of the old three, and three of the new five, can be disjoint. Joint consensus or one-at-a-time changes keep every pair of majorities overlapping."},{"n":19,"ti":"Consistent Hashing","f":"19-consistent-hashing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A hash ring has 10 nodes. An eleventh is added. Roughly what share of the keys move?","o":["Nearly all of them","About one in eleven","Half","None"],"a":1,"w":"Only the keys in the arcs the new node takes over move to it. That is the least any scheme could move."},{"n":19,"ti":"Consistent Hashing","f":"19-consistent-hashing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What do virtual nodes fix?","o":["The cost of each lookup","Hot keys","The need for a hash function","Uneven arcs: with one position each, some nodes own far more of the ring than others"],"a":3,"w":"Many small arcs average out. They also spread a departing node's keys across everyone."},{"n":19,"ti":"Consistent Hashing","f":"19-consistent-hashing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"On a ring with virtual nodes, how are the three replicas of a key chosen?","o":["The next three distinct physical nodes clockwise from the key","The next three positions clockwise, whatever nodes they belong to","Three nodes chosen at random","The key is hashed three times"],"a":0,"w":"Adjacent positions may belong to the same machine, which would put two \"replicas\" on one disk."},{"n":19,"ti":"Consistent Hashing","f":"19-consistent-hashing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is the main cost of rendezvous hashing?","o":["It balances load poorly","It moves most keys when a node changes","Each lookup computes a hash for every node, so it grows linearly with the number of nodes","It needs a large table in memory"],"a":2,"w":"Excellent for tens of nodes. With thousands, a ring's sorted lookup is cheaper."},{"n":19,"ti":"Consistent Hashing","f":"19-consistent-hashing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is jump hash a poor fit for a pool of cache servers?","o":["Its distribution is uneven","It maps keys to bucket numbers 0 to N − 1, so a server can't be removed from the middle without renumbering the rest","It uses too much memory","It is slower than a ring"],"a":1,"w":"It suits numbered shards that are only ever added at the end. Cache servers fail in any order."},{"n":20,"ti":"Partitioning & Rebalancing","f":"20-partitioning-rebalancing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A table is range-partitioned on a key that starts with the event's timestamp. What happens under heavy writing?","o":["Writes are spread evenly over all partitions","Writes are rejected","Every write goes to the last partition, so one node carries the whole write load","The partitions merge"],"a":2,"w":"New keys are always the largest. Put a hashed or bucketed prefix in front to spread them."},{"n":20,"ti":"Partitioning & Rebalancing","f":"20-partitioning-rebalancing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does hash partitioning give up, compared with range partitioning?","o":["Efficient range queries: adjacent keys are scattered, so a scan must ask every partition","Even distribution of keys","The ability to replicate","Lookups by exact key"],"a":0,"w":"The hash that spreads keys evenly also destroys their order."},{"n":20,"ti":"Partitioning & Rebalancing","f":"20-partitioning-rebalancing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In the fixed-number-of-partitions strategy, what happens when a node is added?","o":["Every key is re-hashed","Each partition is split in two","Nothing moves until the node is full","Some whole partitions are reassigned to the new node. Keys never change partition"],"a":3,"w":"The mapping from key to partition is permanent. Only the mapping from partition to node changes."},{"n":20,"ti":"Partitioning & Rebalancing","f":"20-partitioning-rebalancing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"A client's cached routing table says partition 7 is on node A, and it has just moved to node C. What should happen when the client sends a request to A?","o":["A handles the request from its old copy","A sees that it no longer owns partition 7, refuses, and the client refreshes its table and retries","The request is silently dropped","A forwards it and takes ownership back"],"a":1,"w":"The owner check, with the partition's epoch, is what makes stale routing harmless."},{"n":20,"ti":"Partitioning & Rebalancing","f":"20-partitioning-rebalancing.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why is fully automatic rebalancing risky when combined with automatic failure detection?","o":["It uses too much disk","It can't move partitions between racks","A slow node may be judged dead, and copying its data loads the other nodes, which may then look dead too","It conflicts with consistent hashing"],"a":2,"w":"The response to a suspected failure adds load, which produces more suspected failures."}];
